t are current elements, every suffix of every path backwards (an `outgoing` step through `el.in`, an `incoming` one through `el.out`), keeps each element reached whose type the rule applies to, frontier sorted by code point, first-seen order — never filtering on the far types, so a retype on the path cannot hide an owner. Its premise: a rule's verdict on an owner reads the owner and its paths, so if a transition moves the verdict, some path of the owner, on the state before or after, first meets an element the transition dirtied (the stage hooks and the neighbourhood rule add both ends of every relationship they touch and every element they change), and the prefix up to it is on the after state too; walking back from the dirty set on the AFTER state therefore reaches the owner.
 - **`src/service/`** — `Scheduler` (`scheduler.ts`) runs engine work on the host's thread in slices. One pump, two lanes, each in arrival order: the _control_ lane (the replica's own transitions) runs in any state and first; the _model_ lane runs while the scheduler is open. A job is a `read` (one call), a `transition` (one call that changes the model, never interrupted — a transition may run up to 100 ms, AD-23) or a `scan` (a `Steps` generator that `run()` makes). A transition waits for every read that arrived before it and holds everything behind it; at each slice boundary of a running scan, the reads queued before the first transition are answered and other scans are skipped over, never started — so a scan never sees the model move under its iterators (probe: a `Map` iterator that crosses a `clear()` and refill reads everything again, and `Model.elements()` does exactly that on the first ordered read after a rewind). `cancel(id)` removes a queued job or drops a running scan at its next step, unanswered; closed (`setOpen(false)`), the model lane keeps its jobs and a scan that was mid-flight goes back to the head to start over, `run()` called again. The pump reads the injected clock after every unit — a read, a transition, one step — and at `SLICE_TARGET_MS` (8, half of CN-3's 16 ms chunk: at M a collector pause of 5–8 ms lands inside a step now and then) calls `onSliceEnd`, awaits `yieldToHost()` and starts a new slice — `onSliceEnd` also runs when the pump runs out of work, the end of that slice; messages are handled on those host turns only, never inside a unit. Idle and open, it gives its two background slots one step each in turn: the digest check (`setBackground`), whose `restartBackground()` drops its steps in flight (they published nothing) so a new run starts at its next idle step, and the sweep (`setSweep`), whose steps resume across any transition — `restartBackground` leaves it alone and closing only pauses it. A slot empties when its task ends. `paus