 # Replica shell (`frontend/src/lib/engine/`)
 
 The app's side of the replica: plain TypeScript, no runes, no `lib/state/*` import (it may import `lib/api/*`). `lib/state/replica.svelte.ts` wires it into the workspace, and `frontend/README.md` ("Replica (engine shell)") has the detail. The nine model reads can be answered by the replica, one switch per surface, every default `engine` — so the five read surfaces (elements, fuzzy search, incident relationships, containment tree, summary counts) come from the in-browser replica unless `dr.surfaces` says otherwise or the replica cannot serve them, with the server as fallback; a status-bar indicator (`data-testid="replica-indicator"`) shows its state. Two evaluations have a switch each beside them, `navigation` (`evaluateNavigation`) and `criteria` (`searchModel`), both `engine` by default too: a navigation or a criteria search reads the working copy, staged edits and staged artifacts included, and one the engine refuses with a 501 (a script, a pattern it cannot vouch for) is answered by the server and marked. A third switch, `issues` (`getModelIssues`, `validateModel`, the model half of `previewCommit`), is `engine` by default too, gated on the replica's first sweep having ended: on the engine the issues are the replica's live issue store over the working copy, and before the gate opens — or with the switch on `server` — the server answers, as before the flip. `staging` (default `engine`) is its own switch: `staging: engine` FORCES the five read surfaces' switches (`READ_SURFACES`) to `engine` regardless of what `dr.surfaces` set for them, so overriding one of them to `server` needs `staging: legacy` alongside it; `navigation` and `criteria` are never forced, since the server never evaluated staged edits in either mode. The replica also STAGES: with `staging: engine` (the default, `STAGING_DEFAULT`) the user's edits are ops applied to the working copy, and the model store's engine half (`model-engine.svelte.ts`) is a view over its staged batches, mirrored for the synchronous readers — see "The model store" below and `frontend/README.md`'s "State model".
 
