el half from its own staged batches, and when an op is not a model op (artifact, view, `metamodel.move_node`) the server previews those ops alone and the halves merge — counts summed, `structural_blockers` and `issues` concatenated engine first, `would_block` or-ed; no `local` or a rebind is the server's whole request. `previewStaged` passes `{strict: getStrictMode(), batchIds}` and `validateAll` the batch ids, both from `captureStaged()` and only with staging on the engine. A third 501 is answered by the server, never marked: `reaches unreadable rules` (a rule set the engine cannot read: a document its reader refuses, or one that reached it without the server's parse, as every rule set does while the shell sends none). And a 409 that says the call's staged batches, `base_rev` or replica moved — `stale staged batches`, `stale base_rev`, `replica is not ready` — is answered by the server with the whole request, since the replica's rev can lag the shell's `base_rev` and a Validate cut off by a new replica must still answer. `route`'s options also take `shadow?: 'unstaged' | 'always' | 'never'` (default `'unstaged'`: the probe as before; `'always'` hands it `whileStaged: true`; `'never'` runs none) — `comparableWhileStaged(ops)` is `'always'` unless an op is a `create_element` / `create_relationship`, whose id the server mints and the engine never sees; a `validateModel` with nothing staged is `'never'`, since the server's full run names one member of a containment cycle where the engine names every element on it, and `getModelIssues` compares the unstaged state already — and `recheck`: once the engine answers, the side is asked again, and a side gone to the server meanwhile answers from the server, unshadowed, so an issue list a replica answered from a store not yet swept (a call held across a re-bootstrap) is never the caller's. `createEngineSeam(sync, surfaces, shadow?, gates?)` (`seam.ts`): a surface's effective side is `engine` iff its switch says so, its gate (`gates[surface]`, when given) answers true, and the phase is neither `off` nor `server`; `call` is `sync.call`. A closed gate holds nothing: the call goes to the server exactly as with the switch on `server`. The replica store installs it (below).
 - **Shadow comparison — `shadow.ts`, `quiet.ts`.** Dev-only: `shadowEnabled(storage?)` is `import.meta.env.DEV && localStorage['dr.shadow'] === '1'` (absent from a build; a throwing storage or any other value is off). `createShadow({rev, quiet, staged, report})` builds the seam's `shadow`: nothing is compared while `staged()` is true (the replica's answers then hold edits the server has not seen) — asked before `server()` is called, after each `quiet()` and after each re-test round, so a comparison under way ends silently once an edit is staged; the store hands it `anyStaged() || getStagedArtifactDepth() > 0 || hasOverlay()`: `staged-probe.ts`'s `anyStaged`, which asks the engine half's `hasStagedOps()` while it is attached (`attachEngine` sets the probe, `detachEngine` clears it), or an entry in the staged artifact buffer, which the engine's artifact set mirrors and the server has not seen, or the current artifact follower's `hasOverlay()` — after a commit the buffer is empty while the follower still lays the commit's entries (and a created artifact's real-id alias) over the committed artifacts until its refresh lands, or keeps a failed refresh's entries until newer committed news, a state the server does not have. A probe handed `whileStaged` (`route`'s `shadow: 'always'`) is compared with edits staged, since its call sent them to the server too; a 409 on either side, at any point, then ends it silently (the staged batches or the `rev` moved since the call). Otherwise it runs `server()` beside the engine's own outcome, compares by deep equality (object key order ignored, array order not) or, for two failures, the same kind (`ApiError.status`, else the error's `name`) — `summary` compares without `issue_counts`/`undo_depth`, since the engine always answers those `null`/`0`, and `issues` compares its lists as multisets: a list body's `issues`, a bare list and a preview's `structural_blockers` and `issues` are sorted by `JSON.stringify([severity, category, check, message, target_ids, origin])` first, and a list body that is `truncated` drops its `issues` (each side keeps a different subset under the 5,000 cap; `counts` still compare). While the server's own background sweep is still filling its store on a large model, its list lags the engine's and a dev run can report that. Equal: nothing. Different: `await quiet()`, note `rev()`, run `again()` and `server()` once more, note `rev()` again — moved in between → repeat, three rounds at most, then give up silently (a replica that never rests is not a mismatch); a round whose `rev` held still decides it, reporting ONE line (`report`, `console.error` in the store) on a still-different round: `[shadow] <surface> <method> <params>: engine <short> ≠ server <short>`, each `short` cut at 300 characters. An `AbortError` from either side, or an `EngineGoneError` from the engine side (`again()`, or the engine's own outcome), at any point, ends the comparison silently — a `rev` gone to `null` on a `stop()` must never read as two stable, equal reads. `quiet.ts`'s `addQuietProbe(probe)` / `quiet()` is the small registry other stores add a "still settling" probe to. The replica store reaches `shadow.ts` only through a dynamic `import('../engine/shadow')` behind the same DEV check, so a build holds neither the module nor its `[shadow]` string.
 - **The store — `lib/state/replica.svelte.ts`.** `startReplica()` opens the sync and installs `createEngineSeam(sync, readSurfaces())` into `lib/api`, with `sync.settled()` as a quiet probe; `stopReplica()` / `resetReplica()` uninstall both (every read goes to the server again). `subscribeReplicaStatus(listener)` hands every status change with the one before it. With staging on the engine, `startReplica()` also attaches the model store's engine half (`attachEngine(handle)`: the sync's `call` and `on('changed')`, this store's status, `subscribeReplicaStatus`) and `stopReplica()` / `resetReplica()` detach it before the sync stops; with staging on legacy it is never attached (it would move the structure rev on every applied delta, which the legacy half already moves). The switches are read once, at the first start; `resetReplica()` forgets them. In dev, with `dr.shadow` set and some surface on the engine, the seam is installed again with a shadow once the module has loaded (a seam replaced meanwhile is not overwritten). `handReplicaFeed` hands a `reset` event to `feedReset`; the realtime store then does a snapshot event's work minus presence and leases (`refreshSummary()` when ahead, the debounced issues refetch). With `summary` on the engine, `refreshSummary()` keeps the store's issue counts (the engine answers `null`) and asks `refetchIssues()`; `_modelRev` is the answer's either way. `startReplica()` binds the staged artifact buffer to the project it opens (`bindStagedArtifacts`: a buffer staged in another project is dropped silently — no discard listener, which would re-dirty and repoint that project's editor drafts — while the same project's is kept; `boot()` binds it too, beside the other staged families' resets), then builds the artifact follower for the project it opens (its fetches scoped to that project's base, whatever project is active by the time they go), calls `load()`, and pushes the staged buffer when one is kept. The seam's `navigation` gate is the current follower's `loaded()`: a navigation may name artifacts, so until the first load lands (or after a stop, until the next follower's lands) navigations are the server's, as before the flip; `criteria` read no artifact and have no gate. The `issues` gate is `getStagingSide() === 'engine'` (the legacy buffer's edits are not in the working copy), the status's `seeded` and the follower's `loaded()` (until then the engine cannot know it must refuse a `validation_rules` project): until a replica's first sweep has ended — after a divergence, a dead worker or a rebind adoption too — the server answers, and the gate closes the moment the engine's replica leaves `ready` (diverged or closed), since its store went with it. A `frozen` replica keeps `seeded`: its list matches the old-metamodel UI until the adoption re-bootstraps it. With the `issues` switch on the engine, the gate's status half (staging and `seeded`) going from closed to open, or the follower's first load (`onLoaded`) while that half is open, schedules the issues refetch (`scheduleIssuesRefetch()`, moved to `model-shared.svelte.ts`: one 300 ms debounce the feed's triggers share, which `stopRealtime()` disarms), and `startReplica()` subscribes the sync's `changed` events: one whose `issues_version` differs from the last seen schedules it while that half is open (`stopReplica()` / `resetReplica()` unsubscribe). The version is per worker; a new worker's gate opening refetches regardless. The follower's `settled()` is a quiet probe while it runs, so a shadow re-test waits out a payload fetch in flight (a peer's artifact the engine does not hold yet) rather than logging the difference; `stopReplica()` / `resetReplica()` stop it before the sync stops, and a start for another project replaces it. `handReplicaFeed` hands it every `artifact` event and reloads it on a `snapshot` event; `onArtifactCommit` and `onStagedArtifactsChanged` (both subscribed once, at module level) reach the current one. `artifact-edits.svelte.ts` fires `onStagedArtifactsChanged` from every function that changes its buffer, `clearStagedArtifacts` included, and `stagedArtifactsForEngine()` projects it to `WireStagedArtifact`s with `$state.snapshot` copies of names and payloads, since an editor's draft may be a proxy a `MessagePort` cannot clone. `registerViewPlacement` / `forgetViewPlacement` / `forgetViewPlacements` reach the sync: the view store registers the COMMITTED document's `placedElementIds` from `refreshView()` — the fetched one, never the staged ops on top, and before `setState`, since the tree refetches on `_view`'s identity — forgets the old view on a switch, a 404, no view or a failed load, forgets all in `clearViewState()`, and adds a quiet probe pending while a `refreshView()` is in flight. The three debounced searches (`Sidebar/Search.svelte`, `Navigation/ElementStartPicker.svelte`, `Snippet/ElementContextRow.svelte`) abort a superseded `listElementsPage` through its `signal`; an `AbortError` leaves the results as they were.
 - **The model store — `lib/state/model.svelte.ts`, `model-shared.svelte.ts`, `model-legacy.svelte.ts`, `model-caches.ts`.** `model.svelte.ts` is a facade: it re-exports the shared half and, for every entity-half name, dispatches to `model-legacy.svelte.ts` or `model-engine.svelte.ts` by `getStagingSide()` (`replica.svelte.ts`, the `staging` switch in `lib/engine/surfaces.ts`) — `legacy` without a sync, `engine` iff the switch says so and the phase is neither `off` nor `server` (`failed`/`frozen` keep `engine`: a re-bootstrap carries the batches staged there); `resetModelStore` and `validateAll` touch both halves directly, since neither is one entity half's concern, and `setModelApiConfig` always sets the shared half's client config (the engine half has none). `model-shared.svelte.ts` holds what every entity half agrees on — the summary, `model_rev`, the structure-rev counter, the live issue map, rules status and the store error — and `applyDeltaShared(d, has)` is the shared half of a delta's application (the structural formula, the issue splice, the summary patch, `clearOverlay()`), taking a `has(id)` callback so its pre-remap "was this id already cached" check reads the CALLER's cache before the entity half's own remap runs (`structure: false` leaves the structure rev to the caller). `model-legacy.svelte.ts` is the server-mode entity half (the fetched-subset caches, the staged-edits buffer) — frozen, and deleted once the engine half is the only one (sub-project F). `model-engine.svelte.ts` is the engine half, a view over the replica: its caches (the legacy half's names) are filled through the `lib/api` seam — so they hold the staged edits — with none of the legacy guards and no temp-id short-circuit (a temp id the engine 404s is in `getMissingElementIds()`); `emit(op)` / `emitMany(ops)` (one batch, all or nothing; a one-op list coalesces like `emit`) write the op into the caches at once (a create under its temp id with a lite tree item, a delete taking the row out of the tree items too), raise the entity's PENDING count, add a PROVISIONAL entry and post a `stage` transition; nothing the engine says overwrites a pending entity except the `stage` answer that brings its count back to zero (its post-state), so an answer never regresses a newer keystroke, and a refused stage (`getModelError()` `rejected`, the engine's text) reads its entities back and drops what it created. It keeps a mirror of the engine's `staged` / `conflicts` / `stagedDiff`, read again whenever a `changed` event's `staged_version` moves, whenever the replica becomes `ready` (after a re-bootstrap the cached elements the staged and parked batches touch, before and after, are read again: adopting says nothing) and after every `stage` answer — its three calls posted as transitions (in order with the stages, never held for a rev, only in `ready`/`frozen`), a refused read retried once and then REFUSED: a store error, nothing hangs, but `stagedSettled()` rejects with `StagedUnreadableError` and the unstage family (and `popLastStaged()`, false) does nothing until a read lands, since that mirror is not the engine's staged list; the staged-edit readers read the mirror's batches then the provisional entries, an entry leaving when a mirror read issued AFTER its answer lands — and no mirror read starts while an edit waits for its answer (its `changed` comes first), which is what keeps an op from counting twice; `getStagedBatchIds()` / `getStagedConflicts()` are the mirror's, `getStagedDiff()` the engine's committed images against its records. The batches a landed commit carried are COMMITTED (`markLanded`, which checkout calls once the POST answers with `prev_rev` set or `rebound`): every staged-edit reader, `captureStaged()` and `getStagedDiff()` leave them out — the diff drops the entities only they name and a committed delete's cascade — until a mirror read no longer holds them, though a `frozen` or `failed` replica keeps them staged until it is rebuilt and applies the